{
  "teamId": 119,
  "year": 2024,
  "narrative": "The 2024 Los Angeles Dodgers arrived with the most anticipated roster in baseball history, headlined by the transformative signing of Shohei Ohtani, and they wasted no time making a statement. Max Muncy delivered back-to-back clutch performances in the season's opening days, and the offense hummed along behind a loaded lineup that would eventually slug 233 home runs. The early months were a portrait of a juggernaut finding its footing — winning consistently in March, April, and May — even as the bullpen occasionally faltered and the opposition landed haymakers of their own.\n\nThe middle portion of the season told a tale of two halves. June brought some of the team's most electric moments, including a three-run walk-off blast from Teoscar Hernández that swung nearly 0.8 win probability in a single swing. But July was a genuine stumble, the one month where the Dodgers finished below .500 at 11-13. Relievers Evan Phillips and Blake Treinen were torched repeatedly, and costly double plays and opponent triples compounded the damage. Yet even in their ugliest stretch, the talent was undeniable — Freeman's grand slam on July 19 and Hernández's steady bat kept the team from spiraling.\n\nAugust became the great reset, the Dodgers posting their best month of the year at 19-8 and reasserting themselves as the class of the National League. Tommy Edman, acquired at the trade deadline, made an immediate impact. Shohei Ohtani continued his historic season, launching his 53rd home run in late September as the calendar turned toward October. The Dodgers finished 98-64, having outscored opponents by 156 runs, and entered the postseason as rightful favorites.\n\nThe playoffs were a masterclass in clutch performance. Ohtani's three-run shot and Hernández's grand slam powered through the Division Series against San Diego, and the World Series against the Yankees produced one of the most dramatic individual performances in Fall Classic history. Down in Game 1 and staring at a Giancarlo Stanton two-run blast, Freddie Freeman answered with a stunning walk-off grand slam that shifted all the momentum. Anthony Volpe's grand slam in Game 4 provided a heart-stopping Yankees response, but the Dodgers steadied themselves, with Hernández delivering clutch hits in the decisive moments. Los Angeles claimed the championship, capping a season defined by star power, resilience through a July dip, and an October run for the ages.",
